WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.585 --> 00:00:01.843
- No matter how small you start,

00:00:01.843 --> 00:00:04.630
start something that matters.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.634
Begin it.

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:06.637
You have to have vision.

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:08.664
I mean, it's Biblical,
where there is no vision,

00:00:08.664 --> 00:00:10.040
people perish.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.410
You know, if you have a dream,

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.901
never forget, you knock
on a door of opportunity,

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:16.793
that it is work who answers.

00:00:16.793 --> 00:00:18.315
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:18.315 --> 00:00:19.491
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:19.491 --> 00:00:20.728
My one word is believe.

00:00:20.728 --> 00:00:23.144
And I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:23.144 --> 00:00:25.063
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:25.063 --> 00:00:26.549
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:26.549 --> 00:00:28.763
today, we're going to learn
from high-performance expert,

00:00:28.763 --> 00:00:30.840
Brendon Burchard, and my take on his

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.695
Top 10 Rules for Success, Volume Two.

00:00:33.695 --> 00:00:35.169
Rule number one is my personal favorite.

00:00:35.169 --> 00:00:37.279
And I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:37.279 --> 00:00:39.038
And as always, as you're listening,

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:40.802
if something really resonates with you,

00:00:40.802 --> 00:00:42.703
if there's a message that
really holds true to you,

00:00:42.703 --> 00:00:44.610
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.759
and put it in quotes so that other people

00:00:46.759 --> 00:00:47.592
can be inspired.

00:00:47.592 --> 00:00:50.384
And when you write it down,
it's most likely to stick

00:00:50.384 --> 00:00:52.343
with yourself as well.

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:53.345
Enjoy.

00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:56.033
(swishing)

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:58.783
(powerful music)

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.927
- Believe in your ability
to figure things out.

00:01:10.064 --> 00:01:13.292
I think that is the
ultimate belief in life,

00:01:13.292 --> 00:01:14.292
that, you know what?

00:01:14.292 --> 00:01:16.556
I believe that I can
figure out how things work

00:01:16.556 --> 00:01:18.057
and I believe that I can get better.

00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:21.036
That fundamental structure in our mind

00:01:21.036 --> 00:01:23.311
it says, "I believe in those two things."

00:01:23.311 --> 00:01:24.847
That, "I can figure things out.

00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:26.091
"And I can get better."

00:01:26.091 --> 00:01:29.667
enables us for lifelong
improvement and growth.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.450
But without 'em, good luck.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.571
'Cause if you take on a task
that you don't know how to do,

00:01:34.571 --> 00:01:36.040
you say, well, I don't know how to do that

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.040
so I quit.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.555
If you want to be like somebody
who you see as a role model,

00:01:41.555 --> 00:01:42.762
but you don't believe in yourself,

00:01:42.762 --> 00:01:44.053
you say, "Oh, I'm not like that."

00:01:44.053 --> 00:01:45.272
So you quit.

00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:49.231
So without belief as
the underlying element

00:01:49.231 --> 00:01:53.027
of your own mindset,
there's no ability to grow.

00:01:53.027 --> 00:01:54.347
There's no ability to go anywhere.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:57.207
You have to look at this next
stage of your life and say,

00:01:57.207 --> 00:01:59.703
"I believe I can figure it out."

00:01:59.703 --> 00:02:03.393
If you have a great dream
and you don't have any clue

00:02:03.393 --> 00:02:04.405
how to figure it out.

00:02:04.405 --> 00:02:06.633
You still go, "I believe
I can figure it out."

00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:09.322
Which allows you to get up,
and get to work on Monday,

00:02:09.322 --> 00:02:10.716
and start working towards it.

00:02:10.716 --> 00:02:12.666
If you don't believe
you can figure it out,

00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:15.978
you don't have any, what
psychologists call expectancy,

00:02:15.978 --> 00:02:17.371
or even agency.

00:02:17.371 --> 00:02:19.335
And so you won't begin the journey.

00:02:19.335 --> 00:02:22.106
But once you believe that
you can figure it out

00:02:22.106 --> 00:02:24.432
and you can do it, then you'll begin.

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:27.625
So always believe in your
ability to figure things out.

00:02:27.625 --> 00:02:30.777
And the first way you should
not compare yourself to others,

00:02:30.777 --> 00:02:34.297
is in terms of, especially
today, their fans,

00:02:34.297 --> 00:02:37.737
their fame, their
followership, their audience.

00:02:37.737 --> 00:02:38.745
Never do that.

00:02:38.745 --> 00:02:42.298
In my industry, that's
very, very popular to do

00:02:42.298 --> 00:02:43.844
and it's ruined a lot of people's lives

00:02:43.844 --> 00:02:46.271
who don't ever rise up to serve,

00:02:46.271 --> 00:02:48.474
because they think they're
not popular enough.

00:02:48.474 --> 00:02:51.003
It's like you write your first book,

00:02:51.003 --> 00:02:53.929
and you don't have as
many sales as other people

00:02:53.929 --> 00:02:55.686
and you think, "Oh, well,
I don't have the audience."

00:02:55.686 --> 00:02:57.974
Or you go to someone's
fan page and you see,

00:02:57.974 --> 00:02:59.541
"Oh, my gosh, look at their followership,

00:02:59.541 --> 00:03:01.765
"look at how many Instagram
followers they have,

00:03:01.765 --> 00:03:03.077
"how many Twitter followers they have."

00:03:03.077 --> 00:03:05.175
And because you see them so big,

00:03:05.175 --> 00:03:06.537
you think, "Oh, I could never be that."

00:03:06.537 --> 00:03:08.034
so you don't even begin.

00:03:08.034 --> 00:03:09.485
And a lot of people do that.

00:03:09.485 --> 00:03:10.995
They say, "Well, I'm not that big."

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:12.551
So they never start.

00:03:12.551 --> 00:03:15.463
But as I always say, no
matter how small you start,

00:03:15.463 --> 00:03:18.072
start something that matters.

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:19.074
Begin it.

00:03:19.074 --> 00:03:21.160
If I began my career looking at my heroes,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.039
looking at a Wayne Dyer or Tony Robbins

00:03:24.039 --> 00:03:26.819
or Marianne Williamson, a Deepak Chopra,

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:29.128
a Jim Rohn, back in the day,

00:03:29.128 --> 00:03:31.161
or looking at people like Og Mandinos

00:03:31.161 --> 00:03:32.888
who'd sold millions of books,

00:03:32.888 --> 00:03:34.900
or even the famous books that you saw,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.757
millions and millions
and millions of copies.

00:03:36.757 --> 00:03:38.391
If I gauge myself to that and thought

00:03:38.391 --> 00:03:41.743
that was the expectation,
I would never begin.

00:03:41.743 --> 00:03:44.899
So you can't expect to start
out with tons of people.

00:03:44.899 --> 00:03:46.018
Some people say to me all the time,

00:03:46.018 --> 00:03:47.423
"Well, Brendon, it's so easy for you,

00:03:47.423 --> 00:03:49.393
"'cause you got millions of
people on your email list

00:03:49.393 --> 00:03:51.057
"and you have millions
of people on Facebook,

00:03:51.057 --> 00:03:52.817
"and tens of millions on You Tube."

00:03:52.817 --> 00:03:55.213
I say, "It doesn't make
it any harder for me."

00:03:55.213 --> 00:03:57.442
I still show up and shoot this video.

00:03:57.442 --> 00:04:01.134
And the reason I have the
followers and the fans

00:04:01.134 --> 00:04:03.421
and the audience is because I showed up

00:04:03.421 --> 00:04:06.127
and I started when there
was only one dude, 10 dudes,

00:04:06.127 --> 00:04:07.981
hundred dudes, a thousand
dudes watching us.

00:04:07.981 --> 00:04:11.757
My first seminar that I ever
did, we only have 22 people

00:04:11.757 --> 00:04:13.246
in the room.

00:04:13.246 --> 00:04:16.126
And now we fill up
seminars nine times a year,

00:04:16.126 --> 00:04:18.466
often from people from 46
countries around the world

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:20.874
fly in paying thousands
of dollars a ticket.

00:04:20.874 --> 00:04:21.972
And what did I do?

00:04:21.972 --> 00:04:24.461
I started with 22 people in a room,

00:04:24.461 --> 00:04:25.901
and most of those people didn't even pay.

00:04:25.901 --> 00:04:28.685
And every time the guy
walked in with water,

00:04:28.685 --> 00:04:30.671
I counted him as one of those 22.

00:04:30.671 --> 00:04:33.663
(laughs) I had no followership.

00:04:33.663 --> 00:04:37.055
It's never about how big you
start, it's that you start.

00:04:37.055 --> 00:04:39.084
And you get big by staying at it.

00:04:39.084 --> 00:04:41.522
Because what people do is they
put out their first video,

00:04:41.522 --> 00:04:42.532
they write their first book,

00:04:42.532 --> 00:04:44.919
they try their first thing online

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:47.121
and nobody follows, nobody
watches and they quit.

00:04:47.121 --> 00:04:49.132
They get disappointed.

00:04:49.132 --> 00:04:52.076
And where there's disappointment,
there was too high

00:04:52.076 --> 00:04:55.821
of an expectation on
achievement versus learning,

00:04:55.821 --> 00:04:58.107
versus process, versus beginning.

00:04:58.107 --> 00:05:01.339
What you want to do is
never be disappointed.

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:02.730
And the way that you're never disappointed

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.751
is you understand every
single step counts,

00:05:04.751 --> 00:05:06.362
every single step is learning,

00:05:06.362 --> 00:05:07.769
every single step gets you going.

00:05:07.769 --> 00:05:11.497
Greater leaders envision
a compelling and different

00:05:11.497 --> 00:05:14.761
and vibrant future than what is here.

00:05:14.761 --> 00:05:16.758
They have an alternative clear view

00:05:16.758 --> 00:05:18.378
of what the world could be like tomorrow

00:05:18.378 --> 00:05:19.795
than it is today.

00:05:20.958 --> 00:05:22.189
They have a shared purpose.

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:24.781
They believe that they and others

00:05:24.781 --> 00:05:27.470
would be compelled by
interested, inspired by,

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:29.517
and want to work towards.

00:05:29.517 --> 00:05:31.805
And that's a big deal.

00:05:31.805 --> 00:05:33.716
And you always read about
it in leadership, right?

00:05:33.716 --> 00:05:34.830
You have to have vision.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.308
I mean, it's biblical,
where there is no vision,

00:05:37.308 --> 00:05:38.411
people perish.

00:05:38.411 --> 00:05:41.069
We know the power of having that vision.

00:05:41.069 --> 00:05:44.143
So you have to sit down,
though, and actually do it.

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:46.848
The reason we say envision,
versus just have a vision,

00:05:46.848 --> 00:05:50.462
is you have to sit, it's
a practice of envisioning,

00:05:50.462 --> 00:05:53.747
"What should tomorrow
look like for my team?

00:05:53.747 --> 00:05:56.575
"What should tomorrow
be like for my business,

00:05:56.575 --> 00:05:57.741
"for my organization?"

00:05:57.741 --> 00:05:59.819
What should tomorrow
be like for your life.

00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:04.401
And not just tomorrow, a
long-term mindset and view,

00:06:04.401 --> 00:06:08.142
the dream, that magnificent obsession,

00:06:08.142 --> 00:06:11.997
that bold desire, the moon
shot goals and purposes

00:06:11.997 --> 00:06:13.975
and missions of life, the bigger picture.

00:06:13.975 --> 00:06:17.421
That's envisioning a different
reality in the future

00:06:17.421 --> 00:06:18.666
than we experience today.

00:06:18.666 --> 00:06:20.464
And that's what everybody
gets excited about

00:06:20.464 --> 00:06:21.463
in leadership.

00:06:21.463 --> 00:06:23.113
Where there's no vision,
there's no leadership.

00:06:23.113 --> 00:06:25.400
Where there's no vision, people perish.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.510
So we have to envision.

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.429
We always have to continually sit down

00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:30.342
and envision, "Where are we?

00:06:30.342 --> 00:06:31.343
"Where can we be going?"

00:06:31.343 --> 00:06:33.036
It's an active process.

00:06:33.036 --> 00:06:35.823
If you just had a vision one
time and you forget about it,

00:06:35.823 --> 00:06:38.971
it's not going to help you
accomplish the influence

00:06:38.971 --> 00:06:41.293
or the impact you want in your lifetime,

00:06:41.293 --> 00:06:42.741
or in those that you need.

00:06:42.741 --> 00:06:45.194
My whole career has been,
in some way or another,

00:06:45.194 --> 00:06:48.753
a struggle against my own
doubts all along the way.

00:06:48.753 --> 00:06:50.751
As much as people say,
"Gosh, you're so confident.

00:06:50.751 --> 00:06:53.142
"You're the high performance
guy and everything else."

00:06:53.142 --> 00:06:55.039
And I'm like, "Yeah, but it
doesn't mean I don't struggle."

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:58.830
Matter of fact, I probably
put myself in situations

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:01.263
where I must be challenged,
and I must struggle,

00:07:01.263 --> 00:07:03.867
more than any normal sane person.

00:07:03.867 --> 00:07:07.535
(laughs) I'm constantly
pushing myself all the time,

00:07:07.535 --> 00:07:10.958
because I evaluate struggle
as something to be honored.

00:07:10.958 --> 00:07:14.126
It used to be struggle was something

00:07:14.126 --> 00:07:16.140
that was truly honored by people.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.948
They thought that was
something that built character.

00:07:17.948 --> 00:07:20.447
They thought that that was
something that was good.

00:07:20.447 --> 00:07:23.452
And now we've got to be such
a convenient driven society

00:07:23.452 --> 00:07:26.860
that's into ease and
so much just, I think,

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:30.657
an unbelievable level of
engage, I'm sorry, entitlement,

00:07:30.657 --> 00:07:32.384
but people don't think
they have to earn it.

00:07:32.384 --> 00:07:34.416
And I was like, wait, if you have a dream,

00:07:34.416 --> 00:07:37.431
never forget, if you knock
on a door of opportunity

00:07:37.431 --> 00:07:40.363
that it is work who answers.

00:07:40.363 --> 00:07:42.382
(laughs) It takes a lot of work

00:07:42.382 --> 00:07:43.662
to achieve your bigger dreams,

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:46.320
to become the best you who you can be,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.414
to serve more, to create more,

00:07:48.414 --> 00:07:50.032
to contribute at the highest level.

00:07:50.032 --> 00:07:51.146
Man, it's a lot of work.

00:07:51.146 --> 00:07:53.549
So I need you to do your work

00:07:53.549 --> 00:07:56.982
before you respond to the world.

00:07:56.982 --> 00:07:59.227
It's the number one thing
I'm always telling people

00:07:59.227 --> 00:08:00.525
in High Performance Academy

00:08:00.525 --> 00:08:01.884
and all my other training sessions is

00:08:01.884 --> 00:08:04.493
if you are constantly
waking up in the morning

00:08:04.493 --> 00:08:06.564
and all you do is just reply to everybody,

00:08:06.564 --> 00:08:08.271
and you're replying to their text,

00:08:08.271 --> 00:08:09.269
you're replying to their needs,

00:08:09.269 --> 00:08:10.384
you're replying to their emails,

00:08:10.384 --> 00:08:12.334
all of a sudden, the day is ripped away

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:14.527
and you find yourself
out of your own routine.

00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:17.124
But if you set up your morning to win,

00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:19.451
or you set up blocks of time to win,

00:08:19.451 --> 00:08:20.543
as we'll talk about.

00:08:20.543 --> 00:08:24.511
And you do that first, do your work first,

00:08:24.511 --> 00:08:26.139
it's easier to stay disciplined.

00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:27.647
If you're saying, "You know what?

00:08:27.647 --> 00:08:30.177
"This year I want to,
in the next two months,

00:08:30.177 --> 00:08:31.010
I want to be more healthy.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:31.843
Great.

00:08:31.843 --> 00:08:33.590
Get your health stuff out
of the way in the morning.

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:36.920
Create your shake, do your workout,

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:38.839
create a healthy lunch to take to work,

00:08:38.839 --> 00:08:41.272
and you're off and going to a good start.

00:08:41.272 --> 00:08:44.152
But if you wait for the
remainder of the day,

00:08:44.152 --> 00:08:45.767
for discipline to strike,

00:08:45.767 --> 00:08:47.160
it never will.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.485
I know you all have big goals, big dreams,

00:08:49.485 --> 00:08:51.030
big aspirations, just like I do.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:52.952
You've got ambitions to
become an amazing person,

00:08:52.952 --> 00:08:56.624
to build your career, to
create, contribute, to give,

00:08:56.624 --> 00:08:59.029
to be an awesome human being.

00:08:59.029 --> 00:09:03.196
But all of that is impossible
without great habits set up.

00:09:04.358 --> 00:09:07.639
If you don't have routine
and structure set up in a way

00:09:07.639 --> 00:09:09.996
that will keep you on
track, then you'll fall off.

00:09:09.996 --> 00:09:12.322
And having new goals without new habits

00:09:12.322 --> 00:09:15.720
is kind of like having a
new car without wheels.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.671
The habits are the wheels.

00:09:17.671 --> 00:09:21.459
They're the things that make
you able to achieve the goals.

00:09:21.459 --> 00:09:22.953
So you got to have good habits.

00:09:22.953 --> 00:09:25.719
But the challenge for most
people isn't that knowledge.

00:09:25.719 --> 00:09:27.622
I mean, you know you
need to have good habits.

00:09:27.622 --> 00:09:31.037
It's you can't keep
the habit going, right?

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:32.648
Have you ever started it the new year,

00:09:32.648 --> 00:09:33.674
your'e going to get healthier,

00:09:33.674 --> 00:09:35.847
and you start that habit of working out

00:09:35.847 --> 00:09:38.390
more on a more regular
basis and then it goes away.

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:40.953
Or you say, "I'm going to be
more kind and more patient

00:09:40.953 --> 00:09:42.662
"and more awesome to
my partner, my lover."

00:09:42.662 --> 00:09:45.786
And you start being nice
for two or three days.

00:09:45.786 --> 00:09:47.979
And then on the fourth day you're a jerk.

00:09:47.979 --> 00:09:50.209
(laughs) And you're like, "What happened?"

00:09:50.209 --> 00:09:51.772
"I said I was going to be nice to her."

00:09:51.772 --> 00:09:54.859
It's that you didn't set
up the most important thing

00:09:54.859 --> 00:09:57.082
you needed to maintain a habit.

00:09:57.082 --> 00:09:59.334
So what's the biggest secret I've learned

00:09:59.334 --> 00:10:01.886
in almost 20 years in this field?

00:10:01.886 --> 00:10:04.939
It's so simple, but most
people don't have it.

00:10:04.939 --> 00:10:07.402
It's called trigger moments.

00:10:07.402 --> 00:10:11.569
You have to set up trigger
moments to activate your habits.

00:10:12.577 --> 00:10:13.928
What do I mean by that?

00:10:13.928 --> 00:10:17.386
Well, let's say you want
to become a better person.

00:10:17.386 --> 00:10:20.602
You want to become more
kind, more patient,

00:10:20.602 --> 00:10:22.249
more loving with people.

00:10:22.249 --> 00:10:23.901
Now you can just that intention

00:10:23.901 --> 00:10:26.861
or write that down in your
vision, or in your journal,

00:10:26.861 --> 00:10:29.334
or set up on a vision board
that you look at once in awhile.

00:10:29.334 --> 00:10:32.423
But in day-to-day life, it's not enough.

00:10:32.423 --> 00:10:34.953
You need things to trigger
you, to remind you,

00:10:34.953 --> 00:10:38.615
to be that particular
kind of person, right?

00:10:38.615 --> 00:10:40.797
It's almost like if
you have a little angel

00:10:40.797 --> 00:10:44.678
speaking in your ear all day
long to tell you what to do

00:10:44.678 --> 00:10:47.343
and how to be, you'd
obviously become better.

00:10:47.343 --> 00:10:48.544
Well, that makes sense.

00:10:48.544 --> 00:10:50.840
Well, let's use that idea.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:55.286
Let's use that idea by setting
up alarms on our phones

00:10:55.286 --> 00:10:57.446
that trigger us to do the
very things we need to do.

00:10:57.446 --> 00:10:59.364
This is going to be so
basic you're going to laugh.

00:10:59.364 --> 00:11:01.893
And then I'll also tell you
how I've literally talked

00:11:01.893 --> 00:11:03.612
to just to Fortunte 50 CEOs,

00:11:03.612 --> 00:11:05.841
and they said it was the one
thing that changed their life

00:11:05.841 --> 00:11:07.199
the most dramatically.

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:08.835
So let's start with this.

00:11:08.835 --> 00:11:09.668
Let's say I have a goal in mind.

00:11:09.668 --> 00:11:12.341
Let's say you want to become more present

00:11:12.341 --> 00:11:14.525
and calm throughout the day.

00:11:14.525 --> 00:11:17.311
Again you could write
that down on a journal.

00:11:17.311 --> 00:11:18.914
"I'm going to be more present at calm."

00:11:18.914 --> 00:11:20.539
You could meditate in the morning saying,

00:11:20.539 --> 00:11:22.745
"Today, I'm going to be
more present and calm."

00:11:22.745 --> 00:11:25.032
You could start out with good intentions.

00:11:25.032 --> 00:11:27.886
But those fall apart
without a trigger moment

00:11:27.886 --> 00:11:30.158
set up throughout the
day to remind you that.

00:11:30.158 --> 00:11:31.390
So what if you did this?

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.028
What if you just set up on your phone

00:11:33.028 --> 00:11:36.735
three alarms during the
day, with a label that said,

00:11:36.735 --> 00:11:38.370
"Close your eyes,

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.537
"take 10 deep breaths in,
remind yourself to be calm."

00:11:43.759 --> 00:11:46.495
So let's say you're
going through your day.

00:11:46.495 --> 00:11:47.328
It's crazy, right?

00:11:47.328 --> 00:11:48.949
It's 10 a.m. and all of a sudden, bing,

00:11:48.949 --> 00:11:50.127
your phone goes off, you look down,

00:11:50.127 --> 00:11:51.502
and it says, "Close your eyes,

00:11:51.502 --> 00:11:53.700
"Take 10 deep breaths."

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.005
And then again it happens at two o'clock,

00:11:56.005 --> 00:11:58.239
and again it happens at six p.m.

00:11:58.239 --> 00:11:59.969
and again it happens at eight p.m.

00:11:59.969 --> 00:12:01.200
What's going to happen?

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.011
You always look at your phone, don't you?

00:12:03.011 --> 00:12:05.207
It's going to go throughout
the day, you didn't forget.

00:12:05.207 --> 00:12:06.217
And the funniest thing is,

00:12:06.217 --> 00:12:08.311
I've had so many people do this worldwide.

00:12:08.311 --> 00:12:10.855
We're talking about hundreds
of thousands of people

00:12:10.855 --> 00:12:12.905
I've taught to this,
hundreds of thousands.

00:12:12.905 --> 00:12:15.592
And they tell me all the time,
"That changed everything."

00:12:15.592 --> 00:12:17.480
And you'll think once you set it up

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:20.503
that the next day you'll forget about it.

00:12:20.503 --> 00:12:22.091
No, that alarm will go off,

00:12:22.091 --> 00:12:23.821
you forgot you set the dang alarm.

00:12:23.821 --> 00:12:25.093
There it goes off in your face,

00:12:25.093 --> 00:12:26.688
and you're like, "All right.

00:12:26.688 --> 00:12:29.915
"I'm trying to be calmer and more present.

00:12:29.915 --> 00:12:33.568
"Close my eyes, take 10 deep
breathes, reground myself,

00:12:33.568 --> 00:12:34.571
"here were go."

00:12:34.571 --> 00:12:36.637
You can't do it once a day.

00:12:36.637 --> 00:12:39.375
You have to have moments
throughout the day

00:12:39.375 --> 00:12:41.583
that trigger you to
enact the new behavior.

00:12:41.583 --> 00:12:42.974
I know this makes sense to you.

00:12:42.974 --> 00:12:44.847
But it's a challenge for a lot of people,

00:12:44.847 --> 00:12:46.207
'cause they never set those things up.

00:12:46.207 --> 00:12:48.160
So what if you did it with an alarm?

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:50.957
For example, for me, on
the opposite spectrum,

00:12:50.957 --> 00:12:52.875
I don't want to just be
calmer and more present,

00:12:52.875 --> 00:12:54.956
I want more energy throughout the day.

00:12:54.956 --> 00:12:56.427
So here's what I do.

00:12:56.427 --> 00:12:57.452
One of my big trigger moments is

00:12:57.452 --> 00:12:59.937
every time my butt hits a chair.

00:12:59.937 --> 00:13:02.607
I don't care if I'm on a plane, or I was,

00:13:02.607 --> 00:13:03.821
where we're shooting here,

00:13:03.821 --> 00:13:05.498
I wrote a lot of my books here.

00:13:05.498 --> 00:13:06.844
I wrote the Motivation Manifesto here.

00:13:06.844 --> 00:13:08.365
I wrote The Charge here.

00:13:08.365 --> 00:13:11.697
And if I'm going to write,
my butt hits the chair.

00:13:11.697 --> 00:13:13.997
I grab my phone, I open the timer,

00:13:13.997 --> 00:13:15.420
and I set 50 minutes.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:18.461
Now at 50 minutes, the timer goes buzzzzz,

00:13:18.461 --> 00:13:21.218
it starts buzzin' and I
look at that and I go,

00:13:21.218 --> 00:13:22.843
"Oh, that's my trigger to get energy."

00:13:22.843 --> 00:13:25.212
So I stand up, I go get some water.

00:13:25.212 --> 00:13:27.647
I take a glass of water, drink it down,

00:13:27.647 --> 00:13:31.131
I do some stretches,
and I do some exercise,

00:13:31.131 --> 00:13:34.031
just really briefly, or I'll
go for a walk, whatever it is,

00:13:34.031 --> 00:13:35.881
but I'll do something that usually takes

00:13:35.881 --> 00:13:37.726
just two to five minutes.

00:13:37.726 --> 00:13:40.396
Then I'll sit back down,
I'll set that 50-minute alarm

00:13:40.396 --> 00:13:41.501
and I'll work.

00:13:41.501 --> 00:13:44.668
And what that does is every
50 minutes it triggers me

00:13:44.668 --> 00:13:47.863
to change how I'm physically moving,

00:13:47.863 --> 00:13:50.905
it changes my attention,
so that throughout the day,

00:13:50.905 --> 00:13:52.541
I'm continually refreshed.

00:13:52.541 --> 00:13:56.268
So I never have that two,
three, four o'clock time

00:13:56.268 --> 00:13:58.148
where I'm like, argh, you know?

00:13:58.148 --> 00:13:59.149
Why?

00:13:59.149 --> 00:14:00.219
Because I'm triggering my days

00:14:00.219 --> 00:14:02.541
so that my energy is
maintained throughout the day.

00:14:02.541 --> 00:14:03.540
Makes sense, right?

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:04.840
This could be as simple as,

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.196
let's say you want to be
healthier in your life.

00:14:07.196 --> 00:14:10.303
Okay, let's set up some trigger
moments for you in this way.

00:14:10.303 --> 00:14:12.909
Let's say every time
you drop off the kids,

00:14:12.909 --> 00:14:16.561
on the way back to the house,
you stop at the grocery store

00:14:16.561 --> 00:14:17.838
and get some fresh produce.

00:14:17.838 --> 00:14:19.666
That's just a trigger.

00:14:19.666 --> 00:14:22.812
It's like, okay, did one
thing, drop kids off.

00:14:22.812 --> 00:14:26.028
Now tied to that,
triggered from that action,

00:14:26.028 --> 00:14:29.708
drop kids off, is go to health food store.

00:14:29.708 --> 00:14:31.392
Or let's say you want to get
healthier in the morning.

00:14:31.392 --> 00:14:33.805
One of the easiest ways to help people

00:14:33.805 --> 00:14:36.825
maintain a better
exercise program is this.

00:14:36.825 --> 00:14:39.449
Set a trigger, you wake up in the morning,

00:14:39.449 --> 00:14:41.887
your first action is to drink water.

00:14:41.887 --> 00:14:44.765
Put your exercise shoes on.

00:14:44.765 --> 00:14:46.891
Might get dressed if you
were naked, just saying.

00:14:46.891 --> 00:14:49.291
So you get dressed,
(laughs) put your shoes on.

00:14:49.291 --> 00:14:51.742
You go downstairs or you go to the gym

00:14:51.742 --> 00:14:53.472
and that's the trigger.

00:14:53.472 --> 00:14:55.767
You woke up, you do these actions.

00:14:55.767 --> 00:14:58.192
Nothing interrupts those
actions, that's the action.

00:14:58.192 --> 00:15:00.252
And you have to have those setup.

00:15:00.252 --> 00:15:04.161
One of my other favorite
trigger moments to set up

00:15:04.161 --> 00:15:06.415
is door frame triggers.

00:15:06.415 --> 00:15:07.471
Well, you remember that.

00:15:07.471 --> 00:15:09.375
When you walk into a new room

00:15:09.375 --> 00:15:13.551
to have a psychological
trigger go off in your mind,

00:15:13.551 --> 00:15:15.702
then you associated with that doorframe.

00:15:15.702 --> 00:15:17.063
So let me give you an example.

00:15:17.063 --> 00:15:20.192
If I walk into my house at night,

00:15:20.192 --> 00:15:21.888
if I've been working all day,

00:15:21.888 --> 00:15:23.504
and I'm going to walk in,
I'm going to see my lady,

00:15:23.504 --> 00:15:26.943
I have it so whenever I walk
through the doors of my house,

00:15:26.943 --> 00:15:30.287
three words repeat off
immediately in my mind,

00:15:30.287 --> 00:15:31.947
because I've done it so
many times consciously.

00:15:31.947 --> 00:15:33.860
I went to the door, I
said the three words,

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:35.566
said the three words,
said the three words.

00:15:35.566 --> 00:15:37.333
And I did this so many times that now

00:15:37.333 --> 00:15:39.052
when I walk through a door,

00:15:39.052 --> 00:15:41.583
my mind automatically
triggers those three words.

00:15:41.583 --> 00:15:44.272
And I remember to be those three words.

00:15:44.272 --> 00:15:47.853
So what words would you
love to have describe you

00:15:47.853 --> 00:15:50.767
as a person that you
would be happy if that was

00:15:50.767 --> 00:15:52.402
how people described you?

00:15:52.402 --> 00:15:54.130
What would those words be?

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:57.071
Have those and now set
them up in your life.

00:15:57.071 --> 00:15:59.068
I teach executives do this as well.

00:15:59.068 --> 00:16:01.564
I have them have a doorframe trigger

00:16:01.564 --> 00:16:04.368
when they walk into their
office in the morning.

00:16:04.368 --> 00:16:05.936
Soon as they walk through that doorframe,

00:16:05.936 --> 00:16:08.320
they have boom, three
words go through their mind

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.072
about how they want to
be perceived as a leader.

00:16:11.072 --> 00:16:12.976
I have them set up another door frame.

00:16:12.976 --> 00:16:14.401
Every time they walk into a meeting,

00:16:14.401 --> 00:16:15.856
into the conference room,

00:16:15.856 --> 00:16:17.621
when they walk through the
doors of the conference room,

00:16:17.621 --> 00:16:20.472
they have another three words
that trigger off for them.

00:16:20.472 --> 00:16:23.182
And it just reminds them how to be.

00:16:23.182 --> 00:16:25.583
So these aren't big crazy things.

00:16:25.583 --> 00:16:26.593
A lot of people think

00:16:26.593 --> 00:16:28.206
they have to completely change their life.

00:16:28.206 --> 00:16:30.239
What would change your life completely

00:16:30.239 --> 00:16:34.815
is setting up more trigger
moments and associations.

00:16:34.815 --> 00:16:37.102
That when you see something, you do this.

00:16:37.102 --> 00:16:40.504
That when this action is
taken, then that happens.

00:16:40.504 --> 00:16:43.521
That when you have the opportunity

00:16:43.521 --> 00:16:45.537
to set up alarms on your
phone, you set 'em up,

00:16:45.537 --> 00:16:48.956
so that you're interrupted
in your every day life

00:16:48.956 --> 00:16:52.718
to remind yourself to stick to the habits,

00:16:52.718 --> 00:16:54.831
to stick to the intention.

00:16:54.831 --> 00:16:57.441
You do that enough on a continual basis,

00:16:57.441 --> 00:16:59.218
you'll find yourself in so many ways

00:16:59.218 --> 00:17:01.135
completely rejuvenated.

00:17:02.087 --> 00:17:05.314
And I promise, you'll stick
to your habits even more.

00:17:05.314 --> 00:17:09.537
And once you do that you
know that everything changes.

00:17:09.537 --> 00:17:10.864
People don't believe in themselves.

00:17:10.864 --> 00:17:13.398
It's not that there hasn't been evidence

00:17:13.398 --> 00:17:14.898
that they shouldn't believe in themselves.

00:17:14.898 --> 00:17:16.855
They've had some wins,
they've had some goals

00:17:16.855 --> 00:17:18.021
that came true.

00:17:18.021 --> 00:17:19.831
They made the magic happen,

00:17:19.831 --> 00:17:22.687
but they never integrated the win.

00:17:22.687 --> 00:17:25.256
They never had a good thing happen

00:17:25.256 --> 00:17:26.983
and they sat there at
the end of the night,

00:17:26.983 --> 00:17:29.455
thinking about that
good thing that happened

00:17:29.455 --> 00:17:31.729
and allowing it to come into their heart,

00:17:31.729 --> 00:17:35.896
allowing whatever they did to
give themselves credit for it,

00:17:36.783 --> 00:17:39.231
permission to feel good about it and say,

00:17:39.231 --> 00:17:40.349
"You know what?

00:17:40.349 --> 00:17:41.452
"I did this today.

00:17:41.452 --> 00:17:43.072
"It went well today.

00:17:43.072 --> 00:17:44.720
"That worked today."

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:48.074
And to integrate that win,
integrate that success,

00:17:48.074 --> 00:17:51.643
integrate that moment, into
their heart, into their identity

00:17:51.643 --> 00:17:53.803
as a human being.

00:17:53.803 --> 00:17:56.589
What happens is most of us, we
knock off the checklist and,

00:17:56.589 --> 00:17:58.795
"Did it, did it, did it,
did it, did it, did it."

00:17:58.795 --> 00:18:00.708
And five years later, after all these,

00:18:00.708 --> 00:18:02.376
"Did it, did it, did it, made
it happen, made it happen,

00:18:02.376 --> 00:18:03.607
"crushed it, crushed it, crushed it."

00:18:03.607 --> 00:18:05.906
And five years later, we feel
the same about ourselves,

00:18:05.906 --> 00:18:08.169
the same level of confidence,
the same level of belief

00:18:08.169 --> 00:18:10.501
in ourselves, because we
didn't allow those things

00:18:10.501 --> 00:18:15.098
to enter our psyche, enter
our spirit as strength.

00:18:15.098 --> 00:18:16.099
We didn't.

00:18:16.099 --> 00:18:18.445
We had momentum, externally,

00:18:18.445 --> 00:18:20.418
but we didn't have any
momentum internally,

00:18:20.418 --> 00:18:22.215
building our character and our strength.

00:18:22.215 --> 00:18:26.850
We never achieved that
sense of pride or ownership

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:27.856
of what we did.

00:18:27.856 --> 00:18:32.247
We didn't own it that we did
it, so we never go stronger.

00:18:32.247 --> 00:18:33.410
At the end of the day,

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:35.112
just sit down at the
end of the day and go,

00:18:35.112 --> 00:18:36.183
"What worked today?

00:18:36.183 --> 00:18:37.717
"What did I do today?

00:18:37.717 --> 00:18:38.925
"What little thing

00:18:38.925 --> 00:18:40.615
"should I allow myself a
little bit of credit for?

00:18:40.615 --> 00:18:42.440
"What did I say that was nice or kind?

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.527
"What did I send away that I got done?

00:18:45.527 --> 00:18:46.564
"What did I finish?

00:18:46.564 --> 00:18:48.468
"What goal did I move a little closer to?"

00:18:48.468 --> 00:18:51.382
And just at the end of the
day to allow that to come in.

00:18:51.382 --> 00:18:53.835
This isn't about just pepping ourselves up

00:18:53.835 --> 00:18:55.714
and saying, "I'm awesome,
I'm awesome, I'm awesome."

00:18:55.714 --> 00:18:56.547
With no evidence.

00:18:56.547 --> 00:18:57.733
I'm saying, you know,
you got some momentum,

00:18:57.733 --> 00:18:58.970
integrate that now.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:18:59.972
Own that.

00:18:59.972 --> 00:19:01.064
You did the work today.

00:19:01.064 --> 00:19:02.279
You fought the good fight.

00:19:02.279 --> 00:19:03.383
You finished the day.

00:19:03.383 --> 00:19:04.582
You're still alive.

00:19:04.582 --> 00:19:07.554
You know what you need to go do tomorrow.

00:19:07.554 --> 00:19:10.454
Integrate that success,
give yourself that credit.

00:19:10.454 --> 00:19:13.320
You'll develop that strength
of spirit that's so important.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.460
What would I have to
do in social situations

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.956
to be able to develop some confidence.

00:19:17.956 --> 00:19:19.350
What would what I need to do

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:23.979
to develop a deeper competency,
skillset, talent, ability,

00:19:23.979 --> 00:19:27.867
in this new area that will
make me an all around stronger,

00:19:27.867 --> 00:19:29.575
more competent and capable person.

00:19:29.575 --> 00:19:31.319
I think we should focus on the plan

00:19:31.319 --> 00:19:33.983
to develop your strengths much more

00:19:33.983 --> 00:19:35.935
than just stopping some
of the inadequacies

00:19:35.935 --> 00:19:37.120
or the worries.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.691
Those will always be there.

00:19:39.691 --> 00:19:42.027
If you don't have them, the game is over.

00:19:42.027 --> 00:19:44.012
If I ever get to the point

00:19:44.012 --> 00:19:47.867
where I'm backstage and
I'm about to go out onstage

00:19:47.867 --> 00:19:49.500
and perform for thousands of people

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:51.739
who've paid thousands
of dollars to see me,

00:19:51.739 --> 00:19:54.450
who came from 40 plus
countries to be in the room.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:56.934
If I don't have any anxiety to that,

00:19:56.934 --> 00:19:59.454
some performance anxiety,
that's going to tell me

00:19:59.454 --> 00:20:03.703
that's probably the day I
should turn it in, quit, retire,

00:20:03.703 --> 00:20:06.408
'cause it means I don't care enough about.

00:20:06.408 --> 00:20:08.474
I want to experience some anxiety.

00:20:08.474 --> 00:20:09.771
I want to have a little stress.

00:20:09.771 --> 00:20:11.319
We know from high performance studies

00:20:11.319 --> 00:20:13.386
that a little bit of
stress can give that edge

00:20:13.386 --> 00:20:16.834
in performance, as long as it's managed.

00:20:16.834 --> 00:20:20.864
As long as it's utilized as
a tool, to pay attention,

00:20:20.864 --> 00:20:25.031
to focus, to execute,
versus as an excuse to stop.

00:20:27.202 --> 00:20:28.910
So how have you been using anxiety?

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:30.396
As a performance enhancement tool?

00:20:30.396 --> 00:20:32.713
Or an excuse to stop?

00:20:32.713 --> 00:20:35.812
The world's highest
performers use it as a tool.

00:20:35.812 --> 00:20:37.312
They get an edge from it.

00:20:37.312 --> 00:20:38.868
And they say, okay, I'm nervous.

00:20:38.868 --> 00:20:41.626
But that tells me I'm in the
game, I better pay attention.

00:20:41.626 --> 00:20:44.076
And they also develop plans
to develop themselves.

00:20:44.076 --> 00:20:45.898
To strengthen themselves
in all their areas,

00:20:45.898 --> 00:20:50.205
emotionally, socially, financially,
physically, spiritually

00:20:50.205 --> 00:20:52.828
so that those anxieties

00:20:52.828 --> 00:20:56.347
start to diminish as they become more.

00:20:56.347 --> 00:20:58.347
Stop prioritizing easy.

00:20:59.756 --> 00:21:01.673
Stop prioritizing easy.

00:21:03.081 --> 00:21:05.820
A lot of people when they
look at their to-do list

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:08.511
of the day, they just start
working towards the easy things,

00:21:08.511 --> 00:21:09.648
knocking off the easy things.

00:21:09.648 --> 00:21:11.648
They think, "That's going
to get me some momentum,

00:21:11.648 --> 00:21:13.007
"it'll get me into the day,

00:21:13.007 --> 00:21:14.712
"or I'll feel like I'm succeeding."

00:21:14.712 --> 00:21:18.139
And the reality is it's
making them less likely

00:21:18.139 --> 00:21:20.144
to actually do the things that count.

00:21:20.144 --> 00:21:21.653
And how do you know this?

00:21:21.653 --> 00:21:23.616
How do you if you're guilty of that?

00:21:23.616 --> 00:21:26.993
You'll know is if your day feels like

00:21:26.993 --> 00:21:28.132
there's a lot of busy work,

00:21:28.132 --> 00:21:31.502
but there's an absence
of your life's work.

00:21:31.502 --> 00:21:32.686
You know what I mean?

00:21:32.686 --> 00:21:35.612
If you don't feel like you're
activities during the day

00:21:35.612 --> 00:21:38.015
are really creating to your vitality,

00:21:38.015 --> 00:21:40.128
to the contributions you
want to make with the world,

00:21:40.128 --> 00:21:43.888
to doing activities, and
tasks that you really enjoy.

00:21:43.888 --> 00:21:44.944
Whose fault is that?

00:21:44.944 --> 00:21:46.188
Is it the man's?

00:21:46.188 --> 00:21:47.187
Is it the government?

00:21:47.187 --> 00:21:49.263
No, (laughs) it's like
you're setting up your day.

00:21:49.263 --> 00:21:50.784
You could choose what to do with it.

00:21:50.784 --> 00:21:52.708
Maybe you need to look at doing things

00:21:52.708 --> 00:21:54.652
that would support you, your health,

00:21:54.652 --> 00:21:57.067
your vibrance and your
joy, your real growth

00:21:57.067 --> 00:21:59.458
and contribution in a
more significant way.

00:21:59.458 --> 00:22:01.629
If you feel like there's always busy work,

00:22:01.629 --> 00:22:03.023
but it's not your life's work,

00:22:03.023 --> 00:22:06.041
you probably prioritize
things at a larger level

00:22:06.041 --> 00:22:08.127
maybe incorrectly for you.

00:22:08.127 --> 00:22:09.635
Now I don't know this.

00:22:09.635 --> 00:22:10.638
I can't gauge it.

00:22:10.638 --> 00:22:11.527
I mean, I'm just talking to you through

00:22:11.527 --> 00:22:14.277
here now, as I look at you listen to me,

00:22:14.277 --> 00:22:16.983
all I can do is encourage
you to just look and say,

00:22:16.983 --> 00:22:18.504
"How have I been prioritizing?

00:22:18.504 --> 00:22:20.327
"Have I had a structure?

00:22:20.327 --> 00:22:21.659
"Have I had a framework?

00:22:21.659 --> 00:22:25.246
"Have I had ideas about what's
important to me in the world?

00:22:25.246 --> 00:22:27.872
And I always run my
decision-making through those,

00:22:27.872 --> 00:22:29.592
because if you haven't set that up,

00:22:29.592 --> 00:22:31.724
this is the time to do that for yourself.

00:22:31.724 --> 00:22:33.992
- Just because there's
something that's easy to do,

00:22:33.992 --> 00:22:36.558
does not mean it must be done today.

00:22:36.558 --> 00:22:39.031
What I would rather you to do is say,

00:22:39.031 --> 00:22:41.441
"I am going to prioritize things today

00:22:41.441 --> 00:22:45.196
"that will make significant
steps forward in my life."

00:22:45.196 --> 00:22:48.168
These are, I just say,
the opposite of stop,

00:22:48.168 --> 00:22:53.114
or sorry, prioritizing easy
is to prioritize progress,

00:22:53.114 --> 00:22:57.665
things that actually move the
needle of your life forward,

00:22:57.665 --> 00:22:59.998
things that actually contribute

00:22:59.998 --> 00:23:01.589
in ways that you find meaningful

00:23:01.589 --> 00:23:03.336
and significant and fulfilling.

00:23:03.336 --> 00:23:04.937
And you know you deserve that.

00:23:04.937 --> 00:23:06.794
But sometimes you get caught up, right?

00:23:06.794 --> 00:23:09.245
Now you've just been caught
up in everybdoy elses's

00:23:09.245 --> 00:23:10.662
hot mess.

00:23:10.662 --> 00:23:13.030
And your life best became a hot mess.

00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:15.271
It's not because you are,
it's that you're just doing

00:23:15.271 --> 00:23:16.885
the easy things to please people,

00:23:16.885 --> 00:23:18.614
because that's easy,

00:23:18.614 --> 00:23:20.168
"Ah, I'm going to please
some people today."

00:23:20.168 --> 00:23:22.361
But what we need you to do is say,

00:23:22.361 --> 00:23:26.614
"What would significantly move
me towards my dreams today?

00:23:26.614 --> 00:23:29.191
"What three things must happen today

00:23:29.191 --> 00:23:32.906
"that would be real movement forward?"

00:23:32.906 --> 00:23:34.459
Not like little checklists.

00:23:34.459 --> 00:23:37.257
Our whole world has gotten so addicted

00:23:37.257 --> 00:23:38.488
to little tiny checklists,

00:23:38.488 --> 00:23:41.193
but they're not doing anything meaningful.

00:23:41.193 --> 00:23:44.214
They're not checking off,
"I'm living my life."

00:23:44.214 --> 00:23:45.512
(laughs)

00:23:45.512 --> 00:23:46.838
So I want you to get back into,

00:23:46.838 --> 00:23:48.505
"What would really progress me today?"

00:23:48.505 --> 00:23:52.747
Stop prioritizing easy,
start prioritizing progress,

00:23:52.747 --> 00:23:53.897
things that matter.

00:23:53.897 --> 00:23:57.945
I want you to take on
something hard every day.

00:23:57.945 --> 00:23:59.977
You do that, you'll start to find yourself

00:23:59.977 --> 00:24:03.290
getting a little more confident,
a little bit more momentum,

00:24:03.290 --> 00:24:06.343
a little bit more into
that space where you feel

00:24:06.343 --> 00:24:08.617
like you're doing things
you were meant to do.

00:24:08.617 --> 00:24:10.709
And the last big one, you
knew I was going to go there.

00:24:10.709 --> 00:24:11.542
Love.

00:24:12.376 --> 00:24:16.593
You know, you already know
the thing about love yourself.

00:24:16.593 --> 00:24:19.523
And maybe in some ways
you've never allowed that.

00:24:19.523 --> 00:24:21.866
You've never noticed the beautiful things

00:24:21.866 --> 00:24:23.177
you've done in your life.

00:24:23.177 --> 00:24:26.413
So you never allowed
yourself to love yourself.

00:24:26.413 --> 00:24:29.476
But at some point, you've got to recognize

00:24:29.476 --> 00:24:31.452
that there's a love that's beyond you,

00:24:31.452 --> 00:24:34.494
beyond me, and beyond your actions,

00:24:34.494 --> 00:24:37.299
and that love is whatever
brought us here alive today.

00:24:37.299 --> 00:24:39.881
Some would say it's God's
will and God's love.

00:24:39.881 --> 00:24:42.029
Some people it's the
randomness of the universe.

00:24:42.029 --> 00:24:44.686
Some people it's nature,
some people it's whatever.

00:24:44.686 --> 00:24:46.783
But there is something way beyond us.

00:24:46.783 --> 00:24:50.127
And there's a way to access that

00:24:50.127 --> 00:24:51.955
and a way to honor that.

00:24:51.955 --> 00:24:54.179
And the way to honor that is
recognize that you are unique

00:24:54.179 --> 00:24:58.277
about seven billion people, no
one is like you specifically.

00:24:58.277 --> 00:25:02.110
And at least take that
as a moment of saying,

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:05.806
"Okay, I'm meant to live my uniqueness.

00:25:05.806 --> 00:25:10.299
"I'm supposed to love the things
that make me odd and weird.

00:25:10.299 --> 00:25:12.586
"I'm supposed to love the
things that make me strong.

00:25:12.586 --> 00:25:14.336
"I was made this way,

00:25:15.895 --> 00:25:18.078
"so let me honor what's good about that.

00:25:18.078 --> 00:25:20.720
"And what's not good about
that, let me work on that.

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.135
"Let me set a schedule
to get better at that

00:25:23.135 --> 00:25:26.367
"so that I can not only
honor what I've been given,

00:25:26.367 --> 00:25:29.709
"but I can give something
back, too, by getting better."

00:25:29.709 --> 00:25:33.876
And that aspect of doing that
that brings in so much joy

00:25:34.957 --> 00:25:36.363
and so much strength in our life is love,

00:25:36.363 --> 00:25:39.021
loving ourselves, loving the process,

00:25:39.021 --> 00:25:42.663
and of course, the greatest
way to believe it ourselves

00:25:42.663 --> 00:25:46.765
is to love other people that
they become so grateful for us

00:25:46.765 --> 00:25:48.943
that they give us some of that feedback,

00:25:48.943 --> 00:25:52.209
that there's so much
appreciation and love and joy

00:25:52.209 --> 00:25:53.592
in the moment with others

00:25:53.592 --> 00:25:56.495
that you can't help but
just feel that vibe,

00:25:56.495 --> 00:25:58.457
that there's an emotional contagion.

00:25:58.457 --> 00:26:00.698
When other people around
you are feeling loved

00:26:00.698 --> 00:26:03.089
and cared for and excited about life,

00:26:03.089 --> 00:26:05.581
it's hard not to rub
off on ya' a little bit.

00:26:05.581 --> 00:26:06.584
And you start saying, "You know what?

00:26:06.584 --> 00:26:07.965
"Life isn't so bad.

00:26:07.965 --> 00:26:08.798
"And you know what?

00:26:08.798 --> 00:26:10.589
"I deserve to feel good
here right now, too."

00:26:10.589 --> 00:26:11.695
And, you know what?

00:26:11.695 --> 00:26:13.644
You start getting some
momentums, some wins,

00:26:13.644 --> 00:26:14.957
you integrate those wins,

00:26:14.957 --> 00:26:16.490
you get some feedback to get better.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.893
And all of a sudden you start
to believe in yourself again.

00:26:18.893 --> 00:26:23.060
All of a sudden you start
to live the charged life.

00:26:24.867 --> 00:26:26.440
- Thank you guys so much
for watching this video.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:28.312
I really, really, hope you enjoyed it.

